Theory
Manchester Encoding is a digital line coding technique used in computer networks to transmit
binary data over communication channels. It combines clock information and data information
into a single signal, allowing the receiver to stay synchronized with the sender without requiring
a separate clock signal.
Unlike NRZ (Non-Return-to-Zero) encoding, Manchester Encoding guarantees at least one
voltage transition in the middle of every bit period. This transition enables reliable clock
recovery and reduces synchronization errors during data transmission.
Manchester Encoding is widely used in:
● Ethernet (IEEE 802.3)
● RFID systems
● NFC (Near Field Communication)
● Data communication systems
● Digital electronics
Working Principle
In Manchester Encoding, every bit contains a transition at the center of the bit interval.
According to the IEEE 802.3 standard:
● Bit 0 → High to Low transition (represented as 10)
● Bit 1 → Low to High transition (represented as 01)
